1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,040
Imagine it's 2026. You
turn on the television.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,940
It's half past seven in the evening.

3
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:11,487
First, a few minutes of commercials.
Then the news. Then more commercials.

4
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:12,867
Then the weather.

5
00:00:13,174 --> 00:00:17,034
Finally, at eight o'clock, the film
you actually wanted to see begins.

6
00:00:17,794 --> 00:00:19,194
It's two hours long.

7
00:00:19,779 --> 00:00:24,379
Except... commercial break. Another
commercial break. And another one.

8
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:26,893
A two-hour film now finishes
at quarter to eleven.

9
00:00:27,123 --> 00:00:29,893
And you have to get up at six.

10
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:33,093
So somewhere around ten, you give up.

11
00:00:33,433 --> 00:00:35,373
You never find out how it ends.

12
00:00:36,469 --> 00:00:40,609
And that's not even the weirdest
part. You didn't choose the film.

13
00:00:40,929 --> 00:00:43,849
You didn't choose when it
started. You can't pause it.

14
00:00:44,129 --> 00:00:45,669
You can't rewind it.

15
00:00:45,893 --> 00:00:48,933
And if you miss the beginning? Too bad.

16
00:00:49,253 --> 00:00:52,093
Wait until somebody
decides to show it again.

17
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:54,670
Of course, you could have it recorded.

18
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:57,210
People have done that for decades.

19
00:00:57,910 --> 00:01:01,350
First with a VCR and a timer,
today with a set-top box.

20
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:04,110
Then you fast-forward through the ads.

21
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:07,805
But then you're no longer watching
it on the broadcaster's schedule.

22
00:01:08,425 --> 00:01:11,665
You've turned it into something
you can watch the way you want.

23
00:01:12,546 --> 00:01:16,846
The broadcast itself is still
linear television. And honestly...

24
00:01:17,046 --> 00:01:19,906
who would choose to
watch like this in 2026?

25
00:01:21,108 --> 00:01:23,388
Well... millions of people still do.

26
00:01:23,863 --> 00:01:26,943
In the United States,
in May 2025, streaming

27
00:01:27,173 --> 00:01:29,663
finally overtook broadcast
and cable combined.

28
00:01:29,893 --> 00:01:34,723
44.8 percent of TV viewing against 44.2.

29
00:01:36,483 --> 00:01:38,063
Overtook. Barely.

30
00:01:38,443 --> 00:01:42,343
After nearly twenty years of
Netflix, YouTube and everything else,

31
00:01:42,423 --> 00:01:46,103
the old way of watching still
holds almost half of TV viewing.

32
00:01:46,472 --> 00:01:48,632
So why hasn't streaming killed it?

33
00:01:49,152 --> 00:01:52,372
The answer has less to do with
television than you might think.

34
00:01:52,591 --> 00:01:56,071
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

35
00:01:56,691 --> 00:01:58,291
It really helps the channel.

36
00:02:00,881 --> 00:02:02,801
Start with the basic idea.

37
00:02:03,481 --> 00:02:05,481
A broadcaster has a transmitter.

38
00:02:06,281 --> 00:02:08,241
The transmitter sends out a signal.

39
00:02:08,581 --> 00:02:11,481
And every television in
the area can pick it up.

40
00:02:12,034 --> 00:02:15,234
One transmission. Millions of receivers.

41
00:02:16,154 --> 00:02:19,974
The broadcaster doesn't have to know
who is watching, or even how many.

42
00:02:20,646 --> 00:02:24,106
It's an incredibly efficient
way to distribute information.

43
00:02:24,246 --> 00:02:27,286
But it has one unfortunate consequence.

44
00:02:27,726 --> 00:02:30,946
Everybody gets the same
thing, at the same time.

45
00:02:31,477 --> 00:02:34,677
Broadcasting doesn't ask
every viewer what they want.

46
00:02:35,217 --> 00:02:38,357
It asks one question: what
should everyone receive?

47
00:02:39,148 --> 00:02:42,288
The broadcaster decides
what's on, and when it starts.

48
00:02:43,088 --> 00:02:45,968
The viewer decides only one
thing: whether to watch.

49
00:02:46,691 --> 00:02:50,171
For most of television's history,
that wasn't really a problem.

50
00:02:50,491 --> 00:02:52,671
There wasn't much of an alternative.

51
00:02:53,688 --> 00:02:55,348
Then the Internet happened.

52
00:02:55,898 --> 00:02:58,558
Instead of "here is what
we're transmitting, take it

53
00:02:58,788 --> 00:03:01,458
or leave it", you get "what
do you want to watch?"

54
00:03:02,138 --> 00:03:06,578
You choose the programme. You
choose when. Pause it. Rewind it.

55
00:03:06,858 --> 00:03:09,398
Pick the audio language.
Turn on subtitles.

56
00:03:09,478 --> 00:03:11,458
Stop halfway and come back tomorrow.

57
00:03:11,937 --> 00:03:14,177
And this isn't really about commercials.

58
00:03:14,257 --> 00:03:16,797
It's about the direction of control.

59
00:03:17,624 --> 00:03:20,364
Broadcasting pushes content towards you.

60
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:23,184
Streaming lets you pull
it towards yourself.

61
00:03:23,709 --> 00:03:27,009
So for a film on a Tuesday
night, streaming simply wins.

62
00:03:27,709 --> 00:03:29,469
But now change one thing.

63
00:03:30,436 --> 00:03:33,496
Instead of a random film,
it's the World Cup final.

64
00:03:34,296 --> 00:03:36,236
Ten million people want to see it.

65
00:03:36,316 --> 00:03:39,036
And they all want to see it right now.

66
00:03:39,581 --> 00:03:42,561
For a transmitter, that's
no harder than one viewer.

67
00:03:43,201 --> 00:03:45,661
It sends the same signal either way.

68
00:03:45,921 --> 00:03:50,001
Ten million receivers don't
cost ten million times as much.

69
00:03:50,689 --> 00:03:54,409
That's the whole trick of
broadcasting. One to many.

70
00:03:56,799 --> 00:03:58,499
The Internet works differently.

71
00:03:59,399 --> 00:04:02,659
The network has to deliver the
stream to a huge number of viewers,

72
00:04:02,739 --> 00:04:07,279
using servers, caches and network
capacity all the way to their screens.

73
00:04:08,219 --> 00:04:11,039
And every extra viewer
needs a stream of their own.

74
00:04:11,577 --> 00:04:14,297
Engineers have become
remarkably good at this.

75
00:04:14,377 --> 00:04:17,657
But the underlying
problem doesn't go away.

76
00:04:18,288 --> 00:04:20,108
A huge number of people are asking the

77
00:04:20,338 --> 00:04:22,808
network for the same
thing at the same moment.

78
00:04:23,508 --> 00:04:27,508
And when it's happening right now,
nobody can download it in advance.

79
00:04:28,349 --> 00:04:29,549
Sport.

80
00:04:29,905 --> 00:04:31,105
Breaking news.

81
00:04:31,555 --> 00:04:36,055
Election night. Things millions
of people want at the same moment.

82
00:04:36,409 --> 00:04:40,569
And that's exactly what broadcasting
is almost ridiculously good at.

83
00:04:40,955 --> 00:04:43,895
Which suggests we've been
mixing up two different things.

84
00:04:44,614 --> 00:04:46,394
Television is a medium.

85
00:04:47,014 --> 00:04:49,534
Broadcasting is a way of delivering it.

86
00:04:50,488 --> 00:04:53,828
A television channel doesn't need
an antenna on the roof anymore.

87
00:04:54,348 --> 00:04:58,228
It can come by cable. By
satellite. Over the Internet.

88
00:04:59,013 --> 00:05:02,393
In Europe in 2024, more
than half of pay-TV

89
00:05:02,473 --> 00:05:05,073
subscriptions already came over broadband.

90
00:05:05,657 --> 00:05:09,837
But the old-fashioned aerial was still
in use in about two homes in five.

91
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,130
So the old way is declining.

92
00:05:12,210 --> 00:05:14,050
But it's hardly gone.

93
00:05:14,781 --> 00:05:17,301
And streaming didn't even
get rid of commercials.

94
00:05:18,221 --> 00:05:21,541
Many services now offer
cheaper plans with advertising.

95
00:05:22,661 --> 00:05:25,441
Apparently, even the Internet
couldn't escape that.

96
00:05:27,591 --> 00:05:30,391
But traditional television
has one more trick.

97
00:05:30,651 --> 00:05:33,931
And it sounds strange after
everything we've just said.

98
00:05:34,537 --> 00:05:36,797
Sometimes people don't want to choose.

99
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:42,080
You sit down on the sofa. You're
tired. You don't know what you want.

100
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:43,900
And you have thousands of options.

101
00:05:44,659 --> 00:05:45,739
You scroll. And scroll.

102
00:05:45,969 --> 00:05:49,119
Twenty minutes later,
you're still choosing.

103
00:05:49,771 --> 00:05:51,871
Traditional television is different.

104
00:05:52,411 --> 00:05:54,891
You turn it on, and
something is already playing.

105
00:05:55,231 --> 00:05:57,811
Somebody else has chosen for you.

106
00:05:58,297 --> 00:06:02,117
Nobody says radio is broken
because you can't pick every song.

107
00:06:02,797 --> 00:06:04,817
That's not what radio is for.

108
00:06:05,371 --> 00:06:09,471
Linear television can work the
same way. It's not interactive.

109
00:06:09,551 --> 00:06:12,771
It's not personalised. And
that's precisely the point.

110
00:06:14,861 --> 00:06:18,681
And there's one more situation
where broadcasting is hard to beat.

111
00:06:19,331 --> 00:06:21,631
Imagine a major Internet outage.

112
00:06:21,711 --> 00:06:23,931
Or a natural disaster.

113
00:06:24,011 --> 00:06:28,191
Or mobile networks so overloaded
that nothing gets through.

114
00:06:28,753 --> 00:06:30,833
A transmitter with
backup power doesn't need

115
00:06:31,063 --> 00:06:33,873
every viewer to have a
fast Internet connection.

116
00:06:34,673 --> 00:06:37,773
If you can receive the signal,
you can receive the information.

117
00:06:38,753 --> 00:06:40,953
Even a battery radio will do.

118
00:06:41,547 --> 00:06:43,507
That's one reason many
governments and public

119
00:06:43,737 --> 00:06:46,627
broadcasters still keep
the transmitters running.

120
00:06:47,327 --> 00:06:50,647
Broadcasting isn't just an
old way of watching sitcoms.

121
00:06:50,727 --> 00:06:55,087
It's a remarkably robust way
of reaching an entire country.

122
00:06:57,897 --> 00:07:00,077
So, has streaming killed television?

123
00:07:01,158 --> 00:07:04,358
For watching a film whenever
you like? Pretty much.

124
00:07:05,058 --> 00:07:07,178
Why wait until eight o'clock?

125
00:07:07,298 --> 00:07:09,118
Why sit through the interruptions?

126
00:07:09,418 --> 00:07:11,138
Why not watch the rest tomorrow?

127
00:07:11,522 --> 00:07:13,762
But for sending the same
thing to millions of people at

128
00:07:13,992 --> 00:07:17,782
once, broadcasting suddenly
looks much less obsolete.

129
00:07:18,402 --> 00:07:22,562
In fact, it looks almost elegant.
One transmitter. One signal.

130
00:07:22,642 --> 00:07:23,842
Millions of receivers.

131
00:07:25,570 --> 00:07:30,010
We often assume old technology disappears
when something newer comes along.

132
00:07:30,790 --> 00:07:33,510
But radio didn't disappear
because of podcasts.

133
00:07:34,170 --> 00:07:36,810
Books didn't disappear because of e-books.

134
00:07:37,489 --> 00:07:41,909
The old technology moves into a
different role. The one it does best.

135
00:07:43,679 --> 00:07:46,979
So the real question isn't
whether television is dead.

136
00:07:47,619 --> 00:07:51,379
It's whether we need everyone to
see the same thing at the same time.

137
00:07:52,126 --> 00:07:54,546
For a two-hour film? Probably not.

138
00:07:55,251 --> 00:07:57,511
For the World Cup final? Maybe.

139
00:07:58,270 --> 00:08:00,430
For an emergency? Absolutely.

140
00:08:01,710 --> 00:08:05,330
And that's why, even in
2026, you can still turn on

141
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:08,390
a television and find the
same old thing waiting.

142
00:08:09,102 --> 00:08:10,762
A signal. A schedule.

143
00:08:10,842 --> 00:08:12,642
A bunch of commercials.

144
00:08:12,722 --> 00:08:16,862
And millions of other people
watching exactly the same thing.

145
00:08:17,458 --> 00:08:19,998
Not because we haven't
invented anything better.

146
00:08:20,078 --> 00:08:25,128
But because sometimes, sending one thing
to everyone is still very hard to beat.
